1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,120
A fifty-metre superyacht can cost close to
two million dollars a year just to keep
running, before the owner ever sets foot
on board.

2
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:08,974
Not to buy it.

3
00:00:08,974 --> 00:00:11,050
Just to own it, every single year.

4
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:18,786
For decades, brokers used a simple
shortcut: running costs equal about ten
percent of the purchase price a year.

5
00:00:18,786 --> 00:00:19,960
It's a tidy rule.

6
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:24,380
Here's the problem — for a crewed
superyacht, it's mostly wrong.

7
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:33,526
SuperyachtNews found something brokers
don't advertise: a ten-year-old
thirty-metre yacht's running costs don't
fall the way its resale value does.

8
00:00:33,526 --> 00:00:35,330
The boat gets cheaper to buy.

9
00:00:35,330 --> 00:00:37,134
It never gets cheaper to run.

10
00:00:37,134 --> 00:00:44,488
But before any of that running cost even
starts, there's the number that gets all
the headlines: the price tag itself.

11
00:00:44,488 --> 00:00:55,178
A fully custom superyacht build runs
somewhere around one million dollars for
every metre of length — sometimes more,
before you've added a single luxury
feature.

12
00:00:55,178 --> 00:01:08,868
And size doesn't scale the way you'd
expect. The Arsenale and SuperYachtReview
both point to the same pattern: a
fifty-metre yacht doesn't cost twice as
much as a twenty-five metre one. It costs
five to ten times as much.

13
00:01:08,868 --> 00:01:13,140
From the buyer's desk: that number isn't
just steel and engines.

14
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:23,686
By the time you add navigation
electronics, custom interiors, stabilizers
and a full owner's suite, outfitting alone
can be a third or more of the build cost.

15
00:01:23,686 --> 00:01:24,802
And it isn't fast.

16
00:01:24,802 --> 00:01:33,920
A fully custom build typically takes two
to five years from signing to delivery —
which is part of why the price can move
between quote and launch.

17
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:36,965
Not every buyer starts with a new build,
either.

18
00:01:36,965 --> 00:01:45,594
Most superyacht sales are actually
brokerage deals — buying a yacht already
on the water, which sidesteps that
multi-year wait entirely.

19
00:01:45,594 --> 00:01:47,230
The build is the entry fee.

20
00:01:47,230 --> 00:01:52,924
What keeps it running is a different
budget entirely — and it starts with the
people on board.

21
00:01:52,924 --> 00:01:59,372
Industry reporting is consistent on one
thing: crew is usually the single largest
line item.

22
00:01:59,372 --> 00:02:08,342
A captain on a mid-size superyacht can
earn nine to twelve thousand euros a
month, tax-free, with a chief engineer
close behind.

23
00:02:08,342 --> 00:02:11,094
And it's not just salary — it's headcount.

24
00:02:11,094 --> 00:02:22,560
A fifty-metre charter yacht typically
carries somewhere between twelve and
seventeen crew: deck, interior,
engineering and galley, each department
reporting up to the captain.

25
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:31,948
From the buyer's desk: after a decade
sourcing hardware for boats this size, the
line that blows past budget almost never
looks that way in a spreadsheet.

26
00:02:31,948 --> 00:02:33,167
It's not the engine.

27
00:02:33,167 --> 00:02:39,202
It's the electronics, the deck hardware,
the thousand small parts nobody budgets a
real number for.

28
00:02:39,202 --> 00:02:52,196
Running a vessel this size also means the
captain and senior officers need
commercial maritime licenses, not a
recreational boating certificate —
qualifications that take years, and
salary, to earn.

29
00:02:52,196 --> 00:03:01,782
Crew contracts also tend to be seasonal on
charter yachts, which means recruiting,
training and onboarding costs repeat more
often than owners expect.

30
00:03:01,782 --> 00:03:02,831
Then there's fuel.

31
00:03:02,831 --> 00:03:09,825
One active summer season on a fifty-metre
yacht can burn through four hundred to six
hundred and forty thousand dollars.

32
00:03:09,825 --> 00:03:12,040
That's before a single ocean crossing.

33
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:19,115
Insurance alone is estimated to run at
least around fifty thousand dollars a year
for a yacht this size.

34
00:03:19,115 --> 00:03:25,034
On a boat worth tens of millions, that's
the price of just not sinking the owner's
bet.

35
00:03:25,034 --> 00:03:36,839
That insurance figure also splits two
ways: hull cover, insuring the vessel
itself, and liability cover, protecting
the owner if someone gets hurt or
something goes wrong at sea.

36
00:03:36,839 --> 00:03:38,364
Most owners carry both.

37
00:03:38,364 --> 00:03:40,754
Berth fees swing wildly by location.

38
00:03:40,754 --> 00:03:50,514
Two Miami marinas reportedly quoted
between one hundred forty-three and two
hundred thousand dollars a year for one
hundred fifty-seven-foot yacht.

39
00:03:50,514 --> 00:03:52,174
Monaco runs higher still.

40
00:03:52,174 --> 00:03:55,617
Most owners don't hold a superyacht in
their own name.

41
00:03:55,617 --> 00:04:04,352
It's almost always owned through a
corporate structure or offshore company —
mainly for liability and operating
simplicity, not just tax.

42
00:04:04,352 --> 00:04:15,474
Many owners also run a two-season pattern
— Mediterranean summers, Caribbean winters
— which means an ocean crossing twice a
year, each one its own line on the fuel
bill.

43
00:04:15,474 --> 00:04:28,828
Day to day, most of this is actually run
by a yacht management company, not the
owner personally — payroll, maintenance
schedules, compliance, insurance renewals,
all handled for a separate management fee
on top of everything else.

44
00:04:28,828 --> 00:04:42,302
On top of the annual maintenance line,
most yachts also need a haul-out every few
years — pulling the boat out of the water
for hull work, propellers and running gear
— a separate, lumpy cost layered on top of
the yearly budget.

45
00:04:42,302 --> 00:04:50,627
And maintenance — the unglamorous part —
is often estimated at fifteen to twenty
percent of the yacht's value, every single
year.

46
00:04:50,627 --> 00:04:56,952
Compare that to the one or two percent a
typical homeowner budgets, and the gap is
the real story.

47
00:04:56,952 --> 00:05:01,146
There's a cost that doesn't show up on any
invoice: depreciation.

48
00:05:01,146 --> 00:05:09,598
A new yacht typically loses about ten
percent of its value in the first year,
then slows to six to eight percent a year
after that.

49
00:05:09,598 --> 00:05:15,082
Over five years, it can still add up to
forty or fifty percent of the original
price.

50
00:05:15,082 --> 00:05:23,525
Build quality matters here too — yards
with strong resale reputations tend to
hold their value better than the rest of
the fleet.

51
00:05:23,525 --> 00:05:28,565
But however the curve bends, one thing
doesn't change: the boat gets cheaper.

52
00:05:28,565 --> 00:05:29,612
The bills don't.

53
00:05:29,612 --> 00:05:33,064
And it's not just the mothership that
loses value.

54
00:05:33,064 --> 00:05:42,246
The tender — the smaller boat that ferries
guests to shore — depreciates even faster,
often losing half its value within three
years.

55
00:05:42,246 --> 00:05:51,272
Stack every category together, and a
rough, mid-range estimate for a
fifty-metre yacht lands close to two
million dollars a year.

56
00:05:51,272 --> 00:05:52,072
Crew first.

57
00:05:52,072 --> 00:05:55,960
Then fuel, refit reserve, berth and
insurance behind it.

58
00:05:55,960 --> 00:06:08,330
Independent reports put the full range for
a fifty-metre yacht between one point
eight and three point one five million
dollars a year — depending on how hard,
and how often, the owner actually uses it.

59
00:06:08,330 --> 00:06:10,298
This isn't just our math, either.

60
00:06:10,298 --> 00:06:20,076
Ocean Independence estimates roughly two
million euros a year for a
twenty-million-euro yacht — which lines up
almost exactly with the range we just
walked through.

61
00:06:20,076 --> 00:06:22,510
So why do owners still sign up for this?

62
00:06:22,510 --> 00:06:26,344
Some charter the yacht out part of the
year to claw costs back.

63
00:06:26,344 --> 00:06:33,646
Most just treat it like any other luxury:
the bill is the price of the lifestyle,
not a return they expect to see again.

64
00:06:33,646 --> 00:06:45,944
Here's what chartering actually pays: a
fifty-metre yacht typically lists for
somewhere between one hundred eighty and
two hundred thousand euros a week,
climbing to three hundred fifty thousand
in peak season.

65
00:06:45,944 --> 00:06:51,173
But that rate doesn't include fuel, food,
drinks or marina fees during the trip.

66
00:06:51,173 --> 00:07:02,610
Charterers also pre-fund an advance
provisioning allowance — typically thirty
to forty percent of the charter fee on a
motor yacht — and whatever's unspent comes
back to them.

67
00:07:02,610 --> 00:07:12,964
Scale that up, and on a
two-hundred-thousand-euro charter week,
that APA alone can run sixty to eighty
thousand euros — on top of the charter fee
itself.

68
00:07:12,964 --> 00:07:19,453
And even a yacht actively marketed for
charter is rarely booked more than twelve
to fifteen weeks a year.

69
00:07:19,453 --> 00:07:25,262
The rest of the calendar, the bills keep
running with no charter fee coming in to
offset them.

70
00:07:25,262 --> 00:07:30,040
Run the math, and chartering covers a real
chunk of the annual bill.

71
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:34,536
But for most owners, it's a partial
offset, not a profit center.

72
00:07:34,536 --> 00:07:41,710
One more thing from the buyer's desk: the
number on the first quote is almost never
the number on the final invoice.

73
00:07:41,710 --> 00:07:47,770
Change orders, scope creep and currency
swings all show up somewhere between
signing and delivery.

74
00:07:47,770 --> 00:07:59,924
That gap is exactly why every figure in
this video is a range, not a promise — and
why the real number, for any specific
yacht, only exists on that owner's own
invoice.

75
00:07:59,924 --> 00:08:02,845
The purchase price is just the entry
ticket.

76
00:08:02,845 --> 00:08:08,886
The real number shows up every year after
— and on a boat this size, it never really
stops.

77
00:08:08,886 --> 00:08:20,920
None of this works without the marinas,
the crew agencies and the shipyards behind
it — which is exactly where our other two
series, Shipping & Ports and Maritime
Business, pick up the story.

78
00:08:20,920 --> 00:08:27,295
This is Yacht Economics — the real cost
behind the boats, the brands and the
business of the sea.

79
00:08:27,295 --> 00:08:28,610
Below the Waterline.
